WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.960
Emails, WhatsApp replies and business letters that win trust.

2
00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.120
This is the video for module 2 of Business Communication.

3
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:19.490
Email is still the main formal channel for banks, grant programmes, larger companies and government. Your email is often the first thing they see of your business.

4
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:30.060
WhatsApp is often your shop window, your order book and your customer service desk, all in one. Customers decide in seconds whether you are serious.

5
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:37.670
And a formal letter is still needed when you apply for a contract, make a complaint or confirm an agreement.

6
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:49.750
Start with a specific subject line. Hello tells the reader nothing. Request for invoice, Main Hall booking, 29 March, tells them everything before they open it.

7
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.370
Then the body has five parts. First, the greeting. Dear Mrs Okoro for formal, or Good morning Tunde for a known contact.

8
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.450
Second, one sentence saying why you are writing.

9
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.350
Third, only the details the reader needs, in short paragraphs or a list.

10
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.280
Fourth, a clear request: what you want and by when, ideally as a question.

11
00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:24.160
Fifth, your signature: full name, role, business name and phone number. Mention and clearly name every attachment.

12
00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:30.470
Put the person who must act in the To line, and people who only need to know in CC.

13
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:37.070
When you email many customers who do not know each other, use BCC, so their addresses stay private.

14
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:46.260
For example, Ngozi runs a small catering business in Lagos. She emails Mrs Adebayo at a logistics company.

15
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:50.100
Her purpose sentence names who referred her and what it is about.

16
00:01:51.350 --> 00:02:02.550
Her details are short: groups of 20 to 150, about 3,500 naira per pack at current prices, with the menu attached as one PDF.

17
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:12.170
Her request is a clear question with two days to choose from. When she got no reply, she followed up politely in the same email thread.

18
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:19.580
Most customers ask the same things. Is it available? How much? How do I pay? When can I get it?

19
00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.330
A complete reply greets them by name and confirms it is available.

20
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.850
It gives the price and options, explains payment and delivery, and ends with one clear question.

21
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.960
Every extra message you make a customer send is a chance for them to buy elsewhere.

22
00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:47.570
A new customer asks how much the blue necklace is. Halima used to reply, 8k. Then the customer had to ask everything else, and often left.

23
00:02:48.820 --> 00:03:05.720
Now she replies completely. Yes, it is available. It is 8,000 naira. Matching earrings are 3,000 extra. Delivery within Abuja is about 2,000 naira and takes 1 to 2 days, paid by bank transfer. Would you like the earrings too?

24
00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:15.160
The customer says yes, and Halima confirms in writing: a total of 13,000 naira, and asks for the delivery address.

25
00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:21.680
Always confirm every order in one written message with items, total, payment and delivery date.

26
00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:28.560
Before you deliver, check the payment in your own banking app or the SMS alert from your bank.

27
00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.900
A screenshot of a transfer is not proof of payment.

28
00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.120
Never send customers' phone numbers or addresses to other people.

29
00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:45.380
Turn on two-step verification, so no one can take over your business account with a stolen code.

30
00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.300
And use broadcasts for real news, not constant promotion.

31
00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:57.830
For example, Grace owns a printing shop in Enugu. Her new generator stopped working after two weeks.

32
00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.450
Her first letter was angry, with no date, no receipt number and no clear request.

33
00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:14.340
Her new letter, in full block format, names the generator and receipt number, says what happened, and asks for repair or replacement by 28 March.

34
00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:22.540
She closes Yours faithfully, because she opened with Dear Sir or Madam. If you open with a name, close with Yours sincerely.

35
00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:29.080
To remember. Write a specific subject line, and follow the five parts of an email.

36
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:34.720
Give complete WhatsApp replies, confirm orders in writing, and check payments yourself.

37
00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.080
For complaints, give facts, evidence, a fair request and a deadline.

38
00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:54.730
Now practise. Build one complete email with a strong subject line and signature. Set up your WhatsApp Business profile, catalogue and quick replies. And write one formal letter, then improve it with a reviewer's answers.

39
00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.020
Continue with the lessons below.
